===Etymology 1===
From {{affix|en|bark|t1=dog noise|-er|id2=agent noun}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# Someone or something who [[bark#Etymology_2|bark]]s.
#: {{ux|en|My neighbor's dog is a constant '''barker''' that keeps me awake at night.}}
# A person employed to [[solicit]] [[customer]]s by calling out to passersby, e.g. at a [[carnival]].
#* {{quote-magazine|date=2013-06-07|author=David Simpson
|volume=188|issue=26|page=36|magazine={{w|The Guardian Weekly}}
|title=[http://www.guardian.co.uk/books/2013/may/18/falling-upwards-richard-holmes-review Fantasy of navigation]
|passage=Like most human activities, ballooning has sponsored heroes and hucksters and a good deal in between. For every dedicated scientist patiently recording atmospheric pressure and wind speed while shivering at high altitudes, there is a carnival '''barker''' with a bevy of pretty girls willing to dangle from a basket or parachute down to earth.}}
#: {{ux|en|Bob had amassed a considerable stockpile of double entendres from his days working as a '''barker''' for a strip joint.}}
# A [[shelf-talker]].
# {{lb|en|video games}} A video game mode where the action is demonstrated to entice someone to play the game.
#: {{ux|en|The '''barker''' mode of the arcade video game convinced the teenager to spend a quarter.}}
# {{lb|en|slang|dated}} A [[pistol]].
#: {{rfquotek|Charles Dickens}}
#: {{quote-book|author=George MacDonald Fraser|title=[http://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flashman_(novel) Flashman]|date=1969|page=45|passage=''...Parkin, the Oxford Street gunmaker, sent me a brace of '''barkers''' in silver mountings, with my initials engraved—good for trade, I imagine.''}}
# The spotted [[redshank]].

